{{also|jähr}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|jār}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*jērą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*yōr-}} < {{m|ine-pro|*yeh₁r-}}. Compare Dutch {{m|nl|jaar}}, English {{m|en|year}}, Danish {{m|da|år}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/jaː(ɐ̯)/|[jaːɐ̯]|[jaː]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-Jahr.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aːɐ̯|aː|lang=de}}
* {{homophones|ja|lang=de}} {{qualifier|not official standard}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|n|Jahres|gen2=Jahrs|Jahre|pl2=Jahr|Jährchen}}

# [[year]]

====Usage notes====
* The normal plural is '''Jahre'''.
* The unchanged plural ''Jahr'' is rare in formal prose, but may be found in colloquial or poetic language. It is used chiefly after numerals, most often in adverbial constructions such as ''zwei Jahr später'' (“two years later”).
* In colloquial German, a double plural {{m|de|Jahrende}} may be heard in the sense of “ages”, “a very long time”.

